fbsdkpi: allocator: exact ksize, malloc_aligned, realloc zeroing, contigmalloc fixes

- heap blocks record the size that was asked for; malloc_usable_size()/ksize() return it
  (they returned the padded size, so krealloc could hand out stale padding as data)
- ksize()/realloc on contigmalloc memory (every kmalloc above a page) read the word in front
  of the area as a heap header; they now ask the area table, and realloc frees with
  _kernel_free
- realloc with M_ZERO (__GFP_ZERO) zeroes the bytes past the old contents
- malloc_aligned() honours its alignment (it was plain malloc)
- kmalloc above a page with GFP_ATOMIC/GFP_NOWAIT fails instead of blocking in contigmalloc
UPSTREAM: yes.

--- a/src/Kernel/fbsdkpi/freebsd/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_slab.c
+++ b/src/Kernel/fbsdkpi/freebsd/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_slab.c
@@ -231,8 +231,17 @@
 	/* sizeof(struct llist_node) is used for kfree_async(). */
 	_s = MAX(size, sizeof(struct llist_node));
 
+	/*
+	 * SiliconForest: up to a page this is the heap, aligned to the next power of two of
+	 * the size (linux's kmalloc alignment rule) and non-blocking with GFP_ATOMIC or
+	 * GFP_NOWAIT. Above a page linux promises physically contiguous memory, which here
+	 * only a blocking allocation can give: a request that may not wait fails instead
+	 * of sleeping (or of silently returning memory that is not contiguous).
+	 */
 	if (_s <= PAGE_SIZE)
 		return (malloc(_s, M_KMALLOC, linux_check_m_flags(flags)));
+	else if ((linux_check_m_flags(flags) & M_NOWAIT) != 0)
+		return (NULL);
 	else
 		return (contigmalloc(_s, M_KMALLOC, linux_check_m_flags(flags),
 		    0, -1UL, PAGE_SIZE, 0));
